    "en": {
      "title": "20241115 - asleep",
      "heading": "“Improvisation”",
      "body": [
        "What?! The first anniversary?! Has it really been a year already? Time has moved faster than I imagined. I first improvised this in the second half of 2024, while trying to force out original material for Cold Air’s 29 December show. I used the P-125 in Zhongdong Xinhui’s recording room as a MIDI keyboard, fixed some of the rhythm afterwards, and recently rewrote the ending, added two bars of strings, and replaced the guitar patch. By “keyboard guitar,” I mean playing a guitar sound through MIDI. It has a distinctive quality of not even pretending to be the real thing, and was one of my favourite habits in that phase of writing. I had wanted to put “asleep” out for a long time. It may be my earliest conscious attempt to preserve one of these long improvisations; anything earlier is probably too immature. I personally love this recording very, very much.",
        "I have been asking myself whether it is necessary to choose the anniversary deliberately, release an episode, and write something for the occasion. There does not seem to be much to summarise. There was no special project, and I never managed to update continuously. As for the inner history of releasing this radio, earlier episodes have already approached it over and over, through different themes and from different angles, in walls of text. I would like to update steadily—or rather, I want this podcast, this radio, to become a kind of company for whoever listens. I can remember where I was walking when I heard a certain passage, what the landscape looked like, and what troubled me then. When audio becomes anchored to those memories, both the sound and the memory become vivid and easy to retrieve, slowing the feeling of time as much as possible.",
        "“A person’s sense of time depends on the share of their life that a period occupies. For you as a child, the nine years of compulsory education were almost your entire life; the ten years after university occupy only a third of it.”",
        "Life keeps producing encounters, but every one of them—the chance to study or work, a circumstance, another person, opening a new book, deciding to finish a game, hearing a piece and wanting to transcribe, practise, imitate, or write—is countable. They are severely limited opportunities. Yet young people still assume they are immortal. Somewhere beneath consciousness, I still have unlimited time to do everything I want. There are unread books, more major games to finish, pieces to practise, and a whole batch of demos waiting to be improved. But one foreseeable night in some future summer, cool and otherwise random, a full moon will rise. It will complete another phase, existing calmly and unchanged as it has for billions of years, and happen to be seen by some middle-aged person out for a walk. “How many more times will I see the full moon rise?” “Perhaps twenty.”",
        "The more clearly one sees time passing, the certainty of death, and our ignorance of when it will arrive, the more the progress of time exorcises the unconscious thought that one is immortal—that “yet it all seems limitless.” The day on which I clearly recognise that the remaining occasions can be counted is, in a sense, the second derivative of death. Waiting for that day and waiting for death are not essentially different. So there is plenty to do besides wait: many steps to take, many episodes to release, many personal projects to make. Existentialism has a dependable SOP.",
        "Over the past year this little station released seven episodes, and last month its plays passed one thousand. I was honestly astonished. I never intended to circulate these rough improvisations widely; keeping them within a private circle was already the largest act of courage I could manage. Yet people really did choose to open them and listen—to tolerate the frequent lost beats, muddy sound, drifting tuning, and to look inside those flaws for voicings, harmonic turns, dynamics, and improvised structures worth hearing, along with whatever I was trying to express between the bars. For me, that is the entire meaning of taking this step.",
        "I have not had a piano to play lately, so both the radio and other creative work have been painfully slow. I started posting some of the piano videos I recorded in Hong Kong instead. Every work and improvisation depends on long accumulation, and that accumulation comes from listening and practising. Fortunately, the urge to do both is still frequent. Once life settles a little in the second half of the year, I will buy a piano. I cannot keep letting those impulses come to nothing."
      ],
      "date": "11 Aug 2026",
      "note": "vol.8 / improvisation"
    },
