    "en": {
      "title": "20250907 - Tomadou",
      "heading": "“Improvisation”",
      "body": [
        "The second semester of my Hong Kong master’s seems to have passed much faster than the first. Somehow I went all this time without releasing a radio episode and barely noticed. At last I found a little room in the cracks of life. This one is actually from the archive: I played it near the start of the previous academic year, after returning from a boat trip in Sai Kung, and it carries the emotions and vocabulary of that time. I have a great deal sitting in the archive, but until something begins to sound right to me, I have no impulse to release it. Compared with the production logic of “feeling something and playing,” or with the accumulation of practice and transcription, the slowest process is simply learning to like the recording. For a long time I thought this episode was not very good and that publishing it would only invite ridicule. Recently I listened again and thought: no, this is actually rather nice. Every creator goes through this. Better catch the thought and release it quickly. (There are several more “lost gems” like this waiting.)",
        "The recording is old, but I can still talk about something new.",
        "Spring in Guangdong is exasperating. The wet, sticky air produces anxiety and confusion for no apparent reason. Time itself seems blurred by the viscous unease, leaving me tired and feeling that I have let it slip away. Recruitment pressure and this god-awful weather deserve equal blame. The gravest consequence is that people like me, who live by walking, become reluctant to go outside. Existential experience has its hierarchy too; rather than be suffocated alive, I would prefer to stay in an air-conditioned room and drive in GT7.",
        "Walking and playing GT7 feel very similar to me. Both are beautiful stretches reserved for podcasts. One half of me flies lap after lap around the Nürburgring Nordschleife, feeling every corner, line, throttle point, recovery, and prediction. A decision made in half a second produces a completely different response, so my attention has to be welded to the driving. The other half can disappear entirely into the podcast, following every word, every joke and outburst, almost hallucinating a low-pressure form of virtual social life. Conscious experience splits cleanly in two, while memory anchors the halves together. On a certain track I remember which podcast I once heard there; replaying an episode, I remember which car broke which lap record. The experience is almost identical to walking or running. Sustained concentration can even leave me feeling more at ease and more certain of my own existence.",
        "I like these stretches of time—perhaps I am addicted to them. I want a longer commute, want to get off one stop later, forgive an unexplained mistake in GT7 because it gives me an excuse to restart another lap. Once the shield drops, the pressures of life rush back like a tide: the apparently endless rounds waiting in the future, and, worse, the ongoing loop of confusion and “you cannot run away.” Someone at the centre of adversity cannot honestly treat adversity as survivorship bias; nor can someone in favourable circumstances. Despair is as hollow as hope. I do not want to accept confusion as my real life. I do not want “you cannot run away” to be the only thing I can tell myself whenever I face another challenge or another round of pressure. So I think fiercely, train my reason and worldview over long periods, and do everything I can to find a direction that deserves to be called idealistic while preserving the existence of a real self. More than being soaked in the pressure of social Darwinism, I suffer from a confused life whose logic does not hold. Conversely, more than the relief of that pressure, what makes me feel healthy is knowing that what I work to do still overlaps substantially with what I want to do, and that I can keep finding chances to touch real life.",
        "Recruitment season always seems to bring out this same complaining, bitterness, and groaning. Happily, life has not drifted too far below the “healthy” level described above. That is worth celebrating. Once a driver becomes good enough at saving the car, they naturally grow bolder on the throttle. As for the new deviations that follow, all one can do is wait."
      ],
      "date": "20 Apr 2026",
      "note": "vol.6 / improvisation"
    },
